detail.abstract:
Rapid advances in artificial intelligence are transforming labor markets, institutional governance, and lifecourse risk structures. While technological abundance promises increased productive capacity, it simultaneously intensifies occupational volatility, cognitive overload, and intergenerational uncertainty. Existing insurance systems, historically oriented toward mortality risk and episodic disruption, are poorly adapted to these conditions.&nbsp;
<div>
 This article develops an agency-infrastructure framework for reconceptualizing life insurance as a governance-capable institution for stabilizing long-term human agency under technological acceleration. It analyzes how advanced automation reorganizes vulnerability across occupational, cognitive, and demographic domains and evaluates the limitations of conventional insurance architectures in post-AGI environments.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Building on this analysis, the article introduces a typology of continuity, adaptation, resilience, transmission, and sovereignty-oriented insurance architectures and corresponding product families. It further advances institutional governance instruments addressing algorithmic mediation, data sovereignty, distributive equity, accountability, and democratic oversight.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The article concludes by proposing an integrated policy architecture encompassing regulatory reform, capital adequacy standards, data rights, public-private reinsurance mechanisms, and baseline adaptive support. Supplementary contractual and institutional toolkits are provided in online appendices. Together, the analysis establishes a cumulative research and design agenda for aligning insurance institutions with human continuity and democratic legitimacy in machine-mediated societies.
</div>

Econometric analysis is fundamentally organized around variation. Identification, estimation, and inference assign economic meaning primarily to parameters governing marginal responses to observable changes, while components that do not absorb variation—such as intercepts, fixed effects, latent factors, steady states, and residual covariance—are treated as technical objects. This paper argues that this design choice leaves an important dimension of economic inference unexamined: the economic coherence of the state implied by an econometric model.&nbsp;
<div>
 The paper introduces a state–deviation decomposition as an organizing framework for interpreting econometric representations once they are embedded into economic systems. Observed variables are decomposed into state components, representing equilibrium levels and system constraints, and deviation components, representing marginal adjustments and stochastic fluctuations. Standard econometric methods operate primarily on deviation components, while state components are implicitly absorbed into intercepts, residual structures, or latent representations.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Applying this framework to single-equation models, panel estimators, factor models, asset-pricing systems, and structural equation models, the paper shows that many empirically successful specifications are statistically well defined yet economically under-embedded. Intercepts emerge as endogenous equilibrium parameters, residual covariance reflects internal reallocation required to maintain system balance, and factor dependence approximates shared but unmodeled state structure.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The central contribution is to formulate state consistency as a distinct and testable econometric hypothesis. State consistency evaluates whether the state components implied by an econometric model satisfy accounting identities, aggregation rules, and equilibrium relations once embedded into the relevant economic system, complementing standard marginal inference.
</div>
